#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100679 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f9f0b-70dd-5360-9240-2260ff706c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Coefficient in a Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "89e24005-6d6b-5631-9c24-a6eae4e468ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7111" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ at $x = -2$?" :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nP_{2}(x) = f(-2) + f^{′}(-2)(x + 2) + (f^{″}(-2))/(2)(x + 2)^{2}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x+2)^2$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(x)&=\\dfrac{1}{x} \\\\ f'(x)&=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2} \\\\ f''(x)&=\\dfrac{2}{x^3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate $f''(x)$ at $x=-2$. This gives\n\n$$\nf''(-2)=\\dfrac{2}{(-2)^3}=-\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x+2)^2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{f''(-2)}{2}&=\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)}{2}=-\\dfrac{1}{8}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100689 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33159d0b-24b5-587e-89e1-b545c2be59c4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial of a Given Function About a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "feed91bc-ae9e-56db-9b73-a4a4f50a5c89" :question/math-academy-id "e-737" :question/problem "Find the second degree Taylor polynomial of the function $f(x) = e^{2x}$ about $x=1$." :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1$, we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\nP_2(x) = f(1) + f'(1)(x-1) +\\dfrac{1}{2}f''(1)(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1)$, $f'(1)$, and $f''(1)\\mathbin{;}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x) &= e^{2x} & ⟹ & f(1)|= e^{2} \\\\ f^{′}(x)|= 2e^{2x} & ⟹ & f^{′}(1)|= 2e^{2} \\\\ f^{″}(x)|= 4e^{2x} & ⟹ & f^{″}(1)|= 4e^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(1)$, $f'(1)$, and $f''(1)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{2}(x) &= e^{2} + 2e^{2}(x - 1) + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4e^{2}(x - 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2} + 2e^{2}(x - 1) + 2e^{2}(x - 1)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe plot below shows $f(x)$, $P_2(x)$, and $L(x)$. We can see that $P_2(x)$ is a better approximation to $f(x)$ around $x=1$ compared with $L(x)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1177/Source/Images/e831d7a51f7b5c03333fc68a868c8dcf.png)"}]}
